org.bndtools.applaunch
======================

Launches an Eclipse (Equinox) application from a bnd launcher, primarily used to run and
debug Bndtools itself from within Eclipse (see the bndrun files in bndtools.core). It is a
modified version of bndtools.runtime.eclipse.applaunch-0.1.0.jar that Neil Bartlett
originally wrote.

How it works
------------

1. The bundle waits for the bnd launcher service `aQute.launcher.Launcher` to become ready
   (`launcher.ready=true`). The program arguments of the launch (`-runprogramargs`) are
   taken from its `launcher.arguments` property.
2. The arguments are processed like the Eclipse launcher processes them: the Eclipse
   runtime options are turned into framework properties and locations, the remaining
   arguments are published as application arguments through the Equinox
   `EnvironmentInfo` service (`getCommandLineArgs`, `getFrameworkArgs`,
   `getNonFrameworkArgs`).
3. `org.eclipse.equinox.app` is started. It processes the application arguments itself
   (`-application`, `-product`, `-feature`, ...) and asks the registered
   `ApplicationLauncher` to run the selected application.
4. The application is run on the main thread through a `java.lang.Runnable` service with
   the property `main.thread=true`, which the bnd launcher picks up.

Supported Eclipse runtime options
---------------------------------

Program arguments are configured with `-runprogramargs` in the bndrun file; the arguments
are comma separated, one argument per entry:

    -runprogramargs: \
        -application, org.example.application,\
        -data, ${.}/generated/workspace,\
        -consoleLog,\
        -clearPersistedState

The following options are consumed by this bundle, see "The Eclipse Runtime Options" in the
Platform Plug-in Developer Guide:

    Option                          Effect
    -arch <arch>                    osgi.arch
    -clean                          osgi.clean=true
    -configuration <location>       configuration location (*)
    -console [[host:]port]          osgi.console (empty when no port is given)
    -consoleLog                     eclipse.consoleLog=true
    -data <location>                instance (workspace) location (*)
    -debug [options file]           osgi.debug (empty when no file is given)
    -dev [entries]                  osgi.dev (empty when no entries are given)
    -initialize                     the configuration is initialized, no application runs
    -install <location>             install location (*)
    -launcher <location>            eclipse.launcher
    -nl <locale>                    osgi.nl
    -nlExtensions <extensions>      osgi.nl.extensions
    -noExit                         osgi.noShutdown=true
    -os <os>                        osgi.os
    -user <location>                user location (*)
    -ws <ws>                        osgi.ws
    -framework <location>           ignored with a warning, use -runfw
    -vm <path>                      eclipse.vm
    -vmargs <args>...               eclipse.vmargs, everything after -vmargs is consumed
    -noSplash, -showSplash [bitmap], -endSplash <command>, -protect <value>,
    --launcher.appendVmargs, --launcher.overrideVmargs
                                    consumed, there is no splash screen or native launcher

Option names are case insensitive. An option that requires a value but is not followed by
one is passed on to the application, like Eclipse does.

All other arguments, for example `-application`, `-product`, `-feature`,
`-pluginCustomization`, `-noRegistryCache`, `-clearPersistedState` or `-perspective`, are
left to the Eclipse runtime, E4 and workbench layers which process them from the
application arguments.

(*) Locations accept file system paths, URLs and the symbolic values `@user.home/...`,
`@user.dir/...`, `@none` and `@noDefault`. Because the framework is already running when
the arguments are processed, a location can only be applied when it has not been set yet.
This is normally the case for `-data` and `-user`. The configuration and install locations
are always set by the framework; a differing value is ignored with a warning. The same
applies to `osgi.framework`.

Properties that influence bundle resolution (for example `osgi.os`, `osgi.ws`, `osgi.arch`
for platform specific fragments) or framework start-up (for example `osgi.instance.area`
when it must be known before the first bundle starts, `osgi.console`) have to be given with
`-runproperties` in the bndrun file. They are then already in place when the framework
starts.

Tests
-----

Unit tests for the argument processing are in `test/`. The OSGi integration tests in
`org.bndtools.applaunch.test` launch a headless Eclipse application with the bnd launcher:
`programargs.bndrun` passes every kind of runtime option as program argument,
`systemproperties.bndrun` selects the application through `eclipse.product` only.
